WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.127
Hybrid retrieval combines dense semantic scores with sparse keyword scores.

00:00:05.127 --> 00:00:11.922
In this teaching example, we normalize both score arrays and combine them with a weight called alpha.

00:00:11.922 --> 00:00:16.747
If alpha is larger, the system gives more weight to dense retrieval.

00:00:16.747 --> 00:00:21.367
If alpha is smaller, BM25 has more influence.

00:00:21.367 --> 00:00:26.303
This is a simplified version of hybrid search, but it makes the idea clear.

00:00:26.913 --> 00:00:35.370
Why might this help? A question can contain natural-language meaning and an exact technical term at the same time.

00:00:35.370 --> 00:00:43.445
Dense retrieval may understand the overall intent while BM25 protects important exact matches.

00:00:43.445 --> 00:00:46.445
The weight should not be chosen randomly.

00:00:46.445 --> 00:00:50.110
Run evaluation questions and compare the results.

00:00:50.110 --> 00:00:56.440
In production search platforms, hybrid ranking may use more sophisticated fusion methods,

00:00:56.440 --> 00:01:01.382
but the motivation remains the same: use complementary retrieval signals.
